 "sekcje": [
  [
   "Drug Levels and Effects > Summary of Use during Lactation",
   "Topical use of nitroglycerin for anal fissures by nursing mothers appears to have no adverse effects on their breastfed infants. Topical use on the nipples has been used for alleviation of Raynaud phenomenon of the nipples, but only after cessation of breastfeeding. Nitroglycerin should not be used topically on the nipples during breastfeeding. Sublingual and intravenous nitroglycerin have not been studied during breastfeeding. Observe infants for flushing and discomfort after breastfeeding."
  ],
  [
   "Drug Levels and Effects > Drug Levels",
   "Maternal Levels. Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date.\nInfant Levels. Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date."
  ],
  [
   "Drug Levels and Effects > Effects in Breastfed Infants",
   "Forty nursing mothers used nitroglycerin ointment (dosage not specified) topically for the treatment of postpartum anal fissures for durations ranging from 1 use to 12 months of intermittent use. All but 9 of the women reported side effects from therapy, primarily headache, but also dizziness or lightheadedness. None of the mothers reported any side effects in their breastfed infants."
  ],
  [
   "Drug Levels and Effects > Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk",
   "Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date."
  ]
 ]
